   "about": "The political fortunes of Japan’s Socialist and Communist parties and their influence in trade unions through 1948.",
   "summary": "The report traces the Katayama cabinet’s fall, the negotiations that formed the Ashida cabinet, and conflicts over budgets, war bond interest, corruption allegations, and public employees’ labor rights. It examines anti-Communist organizing in unions alongside renewed Communist united-front efforts, then recounts the Showa Denko scandal and Ashida cabinet’s resignation. Its conclusions assess the Socialists’ weakened position and divisions before the 1949 election, and the implications of anti-Communism in labor organizations for Communist influence.",
